# Copy to `.env` and edit. Nothing here is a secret; `.env` is still not for sharing.

# --- Level 0 (required) ------------------------------------------------------
# The folder that holds your music, as an absolute path on THIS computer.
MUSIC_DIR=/srv/music

# Where the stack keeps its own files (database, backups, certificates).
# DATA_DIR=./data

# The numbers of the account that owns the music files (`id -u`, `id -g`).
# PUID=1000
# PGID=1000

# --- Backups of the database: your choice -----------------------------------------
# The database holds accounts, playlists and likes (a few MB). Your music files are NOT
# part of this; backing them up (or not) is entirely up to you, see the guide for options.
# Default: every night at 03:00, the last 7 kept, in ./data/backups (same disk as the data).
# BACKUP_DIR=/mnt/other-disk/encore-backups   # another disk or a network share, if you have one
# BACKUP_COUNT=14                             # how many to keep
# BACKUP_SCHEDULE="30 4 * * *"                # another time (cron syntax)
# BACKUP_SCHEDULE=                            # empty = no automatic backups (you have your own)

# Your time zone, so "03:00" means 03:00 for you.
# TZ=Europe/Paris

# Bump on purpose, read the release notes first. Tested with 0.64.2.
# NAVIDROME_VERSION=0.64.2

# --- Several people on one server (each with their own artists and playlists) --
# Layout:  MUSIC_DIR/_shared  (music everyone may hear)  and  MUSIC_DIR/<name>  (one per person).
# Set this BEFORE the first start; add people with  python3 encore_user.py add <name>
# SHARED_LIBRARY_PATH=/music/_shared

# --- Level 1: a public https:// address, no router change (Tailscale Funnel) -----
# Switch on with:  COMPOSE_PROFILES=funnel   (setup.sh writes it for you: FUNNEL=1 bash setup.sh)
# or for one command only:  docker compose --profile funnel up -d
# Needs a free Tailscale account. The first start prints a sign-in link:  docker compose logs tailscale
# PROXY_TRUSTED_SOURCES=172.16.0.0/12   # written by setup.sh (FUNNEL=1): lets Navidrome see each guest's real address
# PROXY_USER_HEADER=X-Encore-...        # written by setup.sh: a secret name; the stack refuses to start with one but not the other
# TS_HOSTNAME=music          # the machine's name; the address becomes https://music.<your-tailnet>.ts.net
# TS_AUTHKEY=                # a one-time Tailscale key (tskey-auth-...): the reliable way in. setup.sh asks for it and removes it again.
#                            # Without it the first start prints a sign-in link that is only good for ONE MINUTE (then a new one)
# TAILSCALE_VERSION=v1.104.1 # bump on purpose

# --- People: invitations and friend activity (docs/35). Needs the funnel above ---
# Switch on with:  COMPOSE_PROFILES=funnel,people
# The service creates the accounts of people you invite, so it needs the administrator's Navidrome login (it is kept in this
# one service only; chmod 600 this file):
# PEOPLE_ADMIN_USER=
# PEOPLE_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# PUBLIC_URL=https://music.<your-tailnet>.ts.net   # written by setup.sh once remote access is connected
# SERVER_NAME=Encore                               # shown on the invitation page
# Roles (docs/35): the music server's administrators are owners; so are the names listed here (comma-separated). Owners invite people, see
# everybody, change who may invite and remove people, in the app (Your server > People). Everybody else is a guest until an owner decides.
# Needed when your own everyday account is an ordinary one (an administrator sees every person's library), as on the owner's server:
# PEOPLE_OWNERS=owner       # (the older name PEOPLE_INVITERS still works)

# --- Jam: listening together (docs/30, docs/21 step 7). Needs the funnel and an account per person ---
# Switch on with:  COMPOSE_PROFILES=funnel,people,hub   (add hub to whatever you already have)
# Nothing to fill in: the hub asks the music server whether a login is good. It keeps sessions in memory only.
# The first start builds a small program (a few minutes, once).

# --- Level 1b (advanced): your own name and forwarded ports (Caddy) -----------
# Use with:  COMPOSE_PROFILES=https  (several: COMPOSE_PROFILES=funnel,kit)  or  docker compose --profile https up -d
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=
# DOMAIN=music.example.org
# Only Caddy may reach Navidrome then:
# NAVIDROME_BIND=127.0.0.1

# --- Level 3: play any song ---------------------------------------------------
# Use with:  docker compose --profile kit up -d
# MIN_FREE_GB=20
# With several people (SHARED_LIBRARY_PATH above) a song somebody asks for is saved in THEIR folder:
# PEOPLE=1
# A person whose folder has another name (for example an existing collection that is read-only):
# PERSON_FOLDERS=owner=owner-added

# --- Suggestions (docs/47): what each person might like next ---
# Switch on with:  RECS=1 bash setup.sh   (it adds `recs` to COMPOSE_PROFILES; nothing to fill in)
# Needs no account and no key. Each night it reads a private copy of the music server's database (what each person plays and likes) and asks
# MusicBrainz, ListenBrainz and Deezer about the ARTISTS (names only). The owner can switch it off in the admin website and never sees a person's list.

# --- The quality tier (docs/46): the better copy of a song, found on the Soulseek network ---
# Switch on with:  QUALITY=1 bash setup.sh   (it adds `quality` to COMPOSE_PROFILES and makes the keys below; nothing to type)
# It needs the administrator login of the music server in PEOPLE_ADMIN_USER / PEOPLE_ADMIN_PASSWORD (above): only an administrator can
# see where a song's file is. Nothing is sent to the Soulseek network until the owner switches it on in the admin website.
# About 2.5 GB of programs are downloaded the first time, and Lidarr needs about 400 MB of memory.
# LIDARR_API_KEY=          # made by setup.sh
# SLSKD_API_KEY=           # made by setup.sh
# SLSKD_WEB_PASSWORD=      # made by setup.sh
# SOULSEEK_USER=           # made by setup.sh (a new account needs no sign-up); an account you already have works too
# SOULSEEK_PASSWORD=       # made by setup.sh
# QUALITY_DAILY_CAP=40     # albums started per day (the owner can change it in the admin website)

# --- The admin website (docker compose --profile admin up -d; docs/50) ---
# The group id that owns Docker's socket on this machine, so the admin may open the sign-in window (setup.sh fills it in on Linux).
#   Linux:                     getent group docker | cut -d: -f3
#   Docker Desktop (Windows/Mac), or a socket that belongs to root:  0
#   A system that does not let a container use Docker's socket: leave the admin out of COMPOSE_PROFILES, or use the cookies-file
#   route on the YouTube page (Advanced); everything else works the same.
# DOCKER_GID=999
# The browser shown in the page for signing in to Google (a normal Firefox; about 330 MB, downloaded the first time it is needed).
# SIGNIN_IMAGE=jlesage/firefox:v26.09.1
# Which address the admin listens on (default: every address of this machine, so the home network can reach it; 127.0.0.1 = this machine only).
# ADMIN_BIND=0.0.0.0
